  "notes": "The country-torch-song lane — jazz-inflected phrasing over a warm, roomy Nashville band. The harmony is the entry's real differentiator: \"major with more functional harmony than the radio norm — ii–V–I turnarounds and secondary dominants are in-palette,\" so the ii–V–I family carries top weight and the seventh/ninth spread is pushed well above the genre's triad-heavy default. Drums are \"warm and un-compressed; brushes and cross-stick on the ballads, a light two-beat on the uptempo cuts, kick on 1 and 3 throughout,\" with \"fills are jazz-derived (rim-and-tom rather than rock crash)\" — which is the rim/tom perc lane set with the crash absent. The bass is the second differentiator and is quoted: \"genuine voice-leading — inversions and passing tones rather than root-fifth,\" so `followRootsProb` drops far below the genre's boom-chick default and the passing-tone set widens. Melody is \"the most rubato-tolerant in this section: back-phrased entries, held notes over chord changes,\" which is the low quantize strength and the legato articulation. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF] and the entry says the voicings are generalised. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the humanize values, the melodic density and the countermelody ratio — \"back-phrased\" and \"melisma reserved for the final chorus\" are directions, and 0.72 / 2–6 / 0.42 are this author's reading of them.",
